3. Scholarly citation and accurate attribution

For publications and other scholarly dissemination using the software,
experimental methodology, architecture diagram, or recorded measurements,
the project asks authors to:

(a) cite cagra-chunksplice using CITATION.cff and identify the exact source
    digest, repository revision, or archived artifact used;
(b) cite the relevant NVIDIA cuVS/CAGRA, MIRAGE-ANNS, and HNSW works listed
    below and in README.md;
(c) distinguish this PyTorch mechanism experiment from the upstream
    implementations, and attribute each reported result to its actual source;
(d) describe changes to the experimental conditions and avoid presenting the
    supplied single-seed measurements as universal performance guarantees.

These are scholarly attribution expectations, not additional restrictions on
use, modification, commercial use, redistribution, or other MIT permissions.
Failure to include a publication citation does not terminate the MIT grant.
The mandatory MIT copyright/permission notice must still be retained.

4. Upstream references

CAGRA: Hiroyuki Ootomo, Akira Naruse, Corey Nolet, Ray Wang, Tamas Feher, and
Yong Wang. CAGRA: Highly Parallel Graph Construction and Approximate Nearest
Neighbor Search for GPUs. ICDE, 2024.
https://doi.org/10.1109/ICDE60146.2024.00323
https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuvs

MIRAGE-ANNS: Sairaj Voruganti and M. Tamer Özsu. MIRAGE-ANNS: Mixed Approach
Graph-based Indexing for Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search. Proceedings of
the ACM on Management of Data 3(3), 2025; SIGMOD research track.
https://doi.org/10.1145/3725325
https://github.com/dsg-uwaterloo/mirage
The relevant ANN work is not a SIGCOMM or TOCS publication.

HNSW: Yury A. Malkov and Dmitry A. Yashunin. Efficient and Robust Approximate
Nearest Neighbor Search Using Hierarchical Navigable Small World Graphs.
IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence.
https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2018.2889473
https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09320
